Q: Java Spring Boot response entity with map A: <p>Designing an API is bounded to the requirement of the system but for your code there are some suggestion you can use to improve your implementation:</p>
<ul>
<li>There are bunch of redundant data in this implementation for example when you write a code like <code>ResponseEntity.status(404).body(response)</code> you have already set status code by 404 so you don't need to define a <code>response.put(&quot;code&quot;, 404);</code> like this.</li>
<li>We have different HTTP status code which they have specific meaning for example 404 means &quot;not found&quot; so when you are returning <code>404</code> in response, you don't need to have <code>response.put(&quot;message&quot;, &quot;Payment not found&quot;);</code></li>
<li>As I said HTTP status codes have different meaning and codes with 2xx code means success or codes with 4xx or 5xx means error so when you have ResponseEntity with 404 status code, this code means there is an error for request then you don't need to <code>response.put(&quot;status&quot;, &quot;error&quot;);</code> this.</li>
<li>You can use DTO for your service to hide business domain model objects.</li>
</ul>
<p>For example if I want to refactor your sample code it will be like:</p>
<pre><code>@GetMapping&quot;/api/payments/reservation/{idReservation}&quot;)
public ResponseEntity&lt;Payment&gt; obtenerPagoReal(@PathVariable(&quot;idReservation&quot;) int idReservation) {
    Payment pay = dao_interface.findByIdReserva(idReservation);
    if (pago!=null) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(pay);
    } else {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND).build();
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Postman :&quot;status&quot;: 404, &quot;error&quot;: &quot;Not Found&quot;, &quot;message&quot;: &quot;Not Found&quot; in Java A: <p>Your controller mapping expects <strong><code>document</code> as a path variable</strong>, not as a query parameter.</p>
<p>This means Spring expects URLs like:</p>
<pre><code>POST http://localhost:8080/v1/e/E_PC
</code></pre>
<p>But you're sending:</p>
<pre><code>POST http://localhost:8080/v1/e?document=E_PC

That URL would only work if you used @RequestParam:

@PostMapping(consumes = &quot;application/json&quot;, produces = &quot;application/json&quot;)
public ResponseEntity&lt;InputStreamResource&gt; createDocument(
        @RequestParam Dtype document,
        @RequestBody String data) {
    // code
}

Keep @PathVariable

Use:

POST http://localhost:8080/v1/e/E_PC
</code></pre>

Q: C++ lambda init capture of a std::array A: <p>Without a MWE, I can only guess that the warning probably comes from the <em>parameter</em> of the lambda, not the capture value. You should add <code>&amp;</code> in the type of the <code>current</code> array parameter, otherwise those 8 <code>int</code>s will be copied each time you call the function, like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>auto CountsDifferentiator =
[previous = /* ... */](std::array&lt;int, 8&gt;&amp; current) mutable
{
    // ...
};
</code></pre>
<p>Also, consider using <code>const</code> for the same parameter since you are not modifying it.</p>
